      "caseNotes": "FAIRMONT DENTAL PRACTICE — REFERRAL\nPatient: Mr Liam Foster\nAge: 27\nReferred to: Mr Jonathan Pryce, Consultant Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeon\nSeen: 4 September 2026\nAttended alone to discuss the radiograph findings.\n\nReason for referral:\nA well-defined radiolucency associated with an unerupted lower left third molar. Surgical management requested.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nDull pressure and occasional swelling at the left lower jaw over the past four months. No acute pain and no discharge. He describes the swelling as coming and going and had assumed it was related to grinding his teeth.\nEach episode of swelling lasts two or three days and settles without treatment.\nHe has not needed any painkillers.\nNo foul taste, no bleeding and no difficulty opening his mouth.\nNo fevers or night sweats.\nHis partner has noticed that he grinds his teeth at night.\n\nDental history:\nCaries free. Regular attender. The finding was noted on a routine panoramic radiograph taken on 21 August 2026 as part of a general assessment.\nThe wisdom teeth on the right side are fully erupted and functional.\nLast scale and polish in March 2026.\n\nMedical history:\nFit and well. No previous surgery.\nChildhood asthma, outgrown by the age of ten, with no inhaler needed since.\nBroke his collarbone cycling in 2015, managed without an operation.\nNo bleeding problems in the family.\n\nMedications:\nNone.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nExamination:\nMild buccal expansion of the left posterior mandible, palpable but not visible externally. Overlying mucosa intact, non-tender and normal in colour. No egg-shell crackling. Tooth 38 unerupted with no communication to the oral cavity. Tooth 37 vital on testing and not mobile. No lip or chin paraesthesia — specifically asked and denied. No lymphadenopathy.\n\nAdditional findings:\nMouth opening normal at about 48 mm.\nTemporomandibular joints quiet and non-tender.\nMild wear facets on the lower canines, in keeping with grinding.\nAll other teeth sound.\n\nRadiograph:\nPanoramic view shows a well-corticated unilocular radiolucency of approximately 3 cm, attached at the cemento-enamel junction of the impacted 38 and displacing it inferiorly towards the lower border. No root resorption of 37. The inferior dental canal lies in close proximity to the inferior margin of the lesion.\nA panoramic view from 2019 showed 38 unerupted with a normal follicular space.\nDigital images will be sent with this letter.\n\nPlan:\nRefer for surgical exploration, enucleation and histopathology. The patient has been warned about the proximity of the inferior dental nerve and the possibility of altered sensation, and about the risk of jaw fracture given the size of the lesion.\nHe understands the plan and wishes to proceed.\nWritten information about wisdom tooth surgery was given.\nHe would prefer a general anaesthetic if offered but will follow the specialist’s advice.\n\nSocial:\nWorks as a software developer. Lives with his partner. Non-smoker.\nDrinks socially, about six units a week.\nEnjoys hill walking and cooking.\nHas a holiday booked for the second half of November.\n\nAlso noted:\nAsked how long he would need off work. Enquired about wisdom teeth on the right side.\nHe asked whether the practice could provide a fit note and was advised this would come from the treating team or his general practitioner.\nHe mentioned that his partner is due a check-up next month.\n\nPrevious history:\nMild gum soreness behind 37 in 2022 settled with improved cleaning within a week.\nNo previous jaw swellings before this year.\n\nUnderstanding:\nHe knows the lesion is most likely a cyst arising around the wisdom tooth, and that tissue examination is needed to confirm this.\nHe was told that this type of lesion is usually benign.\nHe has read the leaflet and has no further questions at present.\n\nPractical:\nHe works from home three days a week and has flexible hours.\nHis partner can drive him to and from the hospital.\nHe prefers appointments early in the week.\nHe can be contacted by mobile telephone or email.\n\nAlso asked:\nHe asked whether he should cancel a hygiene appointment booked for October.\nHe wondered whether he could keep the tooth after removal.\nHe mentioned that his gym membership renews next month.\nHe asked whether the hospital has a car park, as he may drive himself to the first appointment.\nHe keeps a diary of the swelling episodes and has offered to bring it.\n\nFamily:\nHis father had a wisdom tooth removed in hospital without problems.\nHis sister is expecting her first baby in December.",
      "recipient": "Mr Jonathan Pryce, Consultant Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeon",
      "letterType": "referral",
      "taskInstruction": "Refer the patient for surgical management of a well-defined radiolucency associated with an unerupted lower wisdom tooth."
