      "caseNotes": "GREENWAY DENTAL PRACTICE — REFERRAL\nPatient: Mr Graham Pollard\nAge: 56\nReferred to: Dr Henry Voss, Specialist Prosthodontist\nSeen: 4 September 2026\nAttended alone, at his own request after discussing options at his recall.\n\nReason for referral:\nSevere tooth surface loss with reduced occlusal vertical dimension. Complex prosthodontic management requested.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nWorn, sensitive teeth and concern about what he describes as a collapsing bite, developing over several years and accelerating in the last two. Sensitivity to cold on most posterior teeth. Increasingly dissatisfied with the appearance of the upper anterior teeth, which he says look short.\nSensitivity is mainly with cold drinks and when breathing in cold air.\nNo spontaneous or night-time pain.\nHis wife has noticed him clenching during the day while working at the computer.\nOccasional morning jaw ache, which settles within an hour.\nHe wants a long-term solution rather than further patching.\n\nDental history:\nHeavy bruxist. A hard acrylic stabilisation splint was supplied in 2022 and is worn inconsistently, perhaps two nights a week. Multiple cracked restorations, three replaced in the past eighteen months.\nRegular attender at this practice since 2010.\nLower left first molar root treated and crowned in 2017, and symptom-free since.\nWisdom teeth removed in his twenties.\n\nMedical history:\nGastro-oesophageal reflux disease, on treatment since 2019 but with breakthrough symptoms two or three nights a week. Anxiety.\nReflux was diagnosed after an endoscopy in 2019, which showed mild oesophagitis only.\nAnxiety is managed with talking therapy and has been stable for the past year.\nKnee arthroscopy in 2014 for a sports injury.\nBowel screening up to date.\n\nMedications:\nomeprazole 20 mg daily\ndiazepam 2 mg occasionally\nHe takes the diazepam rarely, about once a month, mainly before flights.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nSocial:\nPreviously drank two litres of cola a day; now reduced to occasional. Non-smoker. Works long hours and identifies stress as a trigger for clenching.\nNow drinks mainly sparkling water at work, with two or three glasses of orange juice a week.\n\nExamination:\nGeneralised tooth surface loss with exposed dentine on all posterior occlusal surfaces. Cupping on the occlusal surfaces of the lower molars. Reduced occlusal vertical dimension with a loss of approximately 4 mm. Several teeth with short clinical crowns, particularly the upper premolars. No active caries. Periodontal status stable, basic periodontal examination 1 in all sextants. Study models mounted and photographs taken today.\n\nAdditional findings:\nTemporomandibular joints quiet, with mouth opening of about 46 mm.\nNo muscle tenderness on palpation today.\nSoft tissues normal.\nPalatal surfaces of the upper anterior teeth smooth and glazed, in keeping with erosion.\nExisting composite restorations on the lower molars stand proud of the worn enamel.\n\nPlan:\nRefer for assessment regarding occlusal rehabilitation, possible increase in vertical dimension and indirect restorations. Reflux control needs optimising first, and I have written separately to the general practitioner about this. Splint wear reinforced.\nHe understands that treatment is likely to be lengthy and staged.\nHe is motivated and keen to proceed once reflux is better controlled.\nStudy models, photographs and recent radiographs can be sent on request.\n\nSocial:\nMarried, two adult children. Works as a project manager.\nPlays golf most weekends.\nHis son is getting married next summer, and he would like his teeth to look better by then.\n\nAlso noted:\nAsked about the likely cost and duration. Enquired about whitening.\nHe asked whether the practice could keep his old splint for reference.\nHe mentioned that his eye test is due and asked for a local optician’s details.\n\nPrevious treatment:\nA soft splint tried in 2020 was abandoned after a few weeks because of discomfort.\nDesensitising toothpaste used since 2023, with partial relief.\nComposite build-ups on the lower incisors were discussed in 2024 but deferred at his request.\n\nUnderstanding and goals:\nHe understands that controlling reflux and grinding is essential to protect any new restorations.\nHis main goals are comfort when eating and a better smile.\nHe accepts that some teeth may need crowns.\n\nPractical:\nHe travels for work about one week a month and would need appointments planned in advance.\nHe prefers morning appointments.\nHe has private dental insurance, which may cover part of the cost.\n\nAlso asked:\nHe asked whether he could be seen at the specialist clinic nearest his office.\nHe asked whether the practice could recommend an electric toothbrush.\nHe mentioned that his wife is also due her check-up.",
