      "caseNotes": "WILLOWBANK DENTAL PRACTICE — REFERRAL\nPatient: Mr Ryan Doyle\nAge: 34\nReferred to: Dr Claire Donovan, Special Care Dentistry Lead\nSeen: 4 September 2026, attending with his key worker\nThe key worker has supported him for four years and knows him well.\n\nReason for referral:\nToothache and a broken tooth in a patient with a learning disability and severe dental anxiety, unable to tolerate examination in general practice.\n\nPresenting complaint:\nPain in the lower right quadrant for about three weeks, reported by the key worker, who has noticed him holding his face and refusing hard food. He has become withdrawn at mealtimes.\nHe communicates mainly with short phrases and some signing, which the key worker interprets reliably.\nHis sleep has not changed.\nHe is drinking well and eating soft foods such as pasta and yoghurt.\nStaff have not noticed any fever or facial swelling.\nStaff have been cutting his meals into small pieces.\n\nDental history:\nAvoids dental care. Last seen five years ago, when treatment was abandoned. Gagging and visible distress on any attempt to examine, including today. He tolerated the chair for approximately two minutes.\nAccording to his sister, he had dental treatment under general anaesthesia as a child.\nHe brushes with support but often refuses to let staff brush his back teeth.\nHe likes an electric toothbrush and finds the vibration calming.\n\nMedical history:\nModerate learning disability. Epilepsy, well controlled, last seizure over two years ago. Lives in supported accommodation with 24-hour staffing.\nEpilepsy was diagnosed in childhood and is reviewed yearly by a neurologist.\nNo other long-term conditions.\nHearing and vision checked in 2025 and normal.\nWeight stable, according to staff records.\n\nMedications:\nsodium valproate 600 mg twice daily\nStaff give his medication, and he takes it reliably with yoghurt.\n\nAllergies:\nNone known.\n\nExamination, limited:\nA broken-down tooth in the lower right quadrant with visible caries, most likely 46. Generalised plaque and gingival inflammation. No facial swelling and no lymphadenopathy. Afebrile. Full charting was not possible and no radiographs could be taken.\nHe allowed a brief look with a mirror while sitting in the waiting room rather than the dental chair.\nThe key worker held his hand throughout, which helped him settle.\n\nCapacity and consent:\nAssessed today as lacking capacity to consent to dental treatment. A best-interests process will be required, involving the key worker, his sister as next of kin, and the supported living team. Documentation started and enclosed.\nHis sister has been contacted by telephone and supports the referral.\nShe would like to attend the best-interests meeting.\n\nPlan:\nRefer for assessment with additional support, likely requiring intravenous sedation, alongside a desensitisation approach over several visits. Preventive advice given to the key worker, including supervised brushing and a high-fluoride toothpaste, which has been prescribed today.\nHe copes best with early morning appointments and a quiet waiting area.\nWhere possible, staff will visit unfamiliar settings with him beforehand to reduce distress.\nA picture-based story about visiting the dentist has been offered to the staff team.\nNo recent platelet count is on record. A full blood count will be needed before any extraction, as sodium valproate can lower the platelet count and impair platelet function.\n\nSocial:\nAttends a day centre three days a week. Enjoys music and swimming.\nListening to music on headphones helps him stay calm in waiting rooms.\nHe goes swimming every Saturday with a support worker.\nHe has a good relationship with his sister, who visits every fortnight.\n\nAlso noted:\nThe key worker asked about sugar-free alternatives to his usual drinks. He is due an annual health check.\nThe key worker also asked whether parking is available at the hospital site.\nStaff mentioned that his room is being redecorated next month.\nHis flu vaccination was given at the surgery last week.\n\nPractical arrangements:\nHe travels in the supported living service’s own vehicle and needs a familiar driver.\nStaff need at least a week’s notice of any appointment to arrange cover.\nThe supported living manager is the main contact for booking.\nA hospital passport is available and will be brought to the first visit.\n\nPrevious episode:\nA sore upper tooth in 2023 settled after staff increased supported brushing, and no treatment was needed.\n\nAlso asked:\nHis sister asked whether letters could be copied to her.\nStaff asked for a leaflet about tooth care for adults with learning disabilities.\nDay centre staff report that he enjoys art sessions and is settled there.\nHis sister works shifts and is easiest to reach by text message.\nHe has an annual review with his social worker in November.",
